---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Dental clinic network, 6 locations across Lombardy and Piedmont' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Ongoing relationship since 2022 — detailed data 2024–2025' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Digital Advertising Specialist: strategy, campaigns, reporting, team training' },
  { label: 'Channels', value: 'Meta Ads (primary), Google Ads Search' },
  { label: 'Team', value: 'Project manager, videographer, front-desk staff at each location' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '13.55×', label: '2024 program ROAS' },
  { metric: '293', label: 'leads generated in 2024' },
  { metric: '~19.9×', label: 'best single event ROAS' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Turning a recurring events format into a measurable acquisition channel"
  description="Case study: an Open Day program across a multi-location network, measured from the first click to the acquired patient. Meta Ads, 13.55x ROAS."
  eyebrow="Meta Ads · End-to-end measurement"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2>The context</h2>
  <p>
    The client is a dental network with 6 locations that runs a recurring format — themed
    "Open Days" (implantology, orthodontics, gnathology, cosmetic dentistry) — held periodically
    across the different locations. Not a one-off campaign: an ongoing program, repeated several
    times a year, in different cities.
  </p>

  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Counting submitted forms is easy. The client's real question was different: how many of those
    leads actually become patients, event by event, location by location? And why does the same
    format perform so differently depending on where and when it runs?
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    Each Open Day was conceived together with the project manager and the team; photo and video
    material was produced with the videographer. From there on, management was entirely mine:
    building and optimizing Meta campaigns for each individual event and location, periodic
    reporting to the client and — something I particularly care about — training the front-desk
    staff at each location on handling incoming leads, because the best campaign is worthless if
    the contact isn't handled well.
  </p>

  <h2>Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    Cost per lead varied enormously between locations: from €6.50 to over €80 in the same period.
    A figure like that, read only in aggregate, would have gone unnoticed — analyzed location by
    location, it showed how much location, timing and local saturation affected the format's
    performance, just as much as the creative did.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    A stated limitation: the choice of where and when to hold events was up to the brand, not
    advertising management. Geographic budget allocation was therefore not a lever I could
    optimize — distinguishing what you control from what you're subject to is part of the
    analysis work.
  </p>

  <h2>Execution</h2>
  <p>
    Over 16 lead-gen campaigns dedicated to individual events between 2022 and 2026, each with
    creative and budget adapted to the location. In parallel: low-cost local awareness campaigns
    (over 1.9 million people reached with roughly €1,100 total) and, over time, automation flows
    to route leads to the front desks, which helped improve ROAS.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>
    In 2024 the program generated 293 leads with €2,838.64 in spend and €38,473 in attributed
    revenue: a ROAS of roughly 13.55×. The single most efficient event closed at around 19.9× with
    under €800 invested. The 2025 program (partial data as of May) had already generated 192
    leads, with the pipeline still closing.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    To manage a growing average spend over time. To build process, not just campaigns: lead
    automation and front-desk training weighed on the result as much as media optimization did.
    And if I could do it again, I'd structure the content creation process first: recognizing and
    quickly reproducing winning creative would have made it possible to scale the best results
    instead of starting from zero every time.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
